WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.380
- Podcast station, what is good?

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.260
A very special episode today.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.660
I believe this man will have
no need for an introduction.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.820
We have Mark Zuckerberg.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.340
I have to start practicing this, Mark.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.623
I've been in a bunch of
meetings saying Meta,

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.323
just a lot of practice.

00:00:15.323 --> 00:00:16.560
You really threw me for a curve ball.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.910
- Yeah, I'm still getting used to it too.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:23.200
- So obviously Mark is at the helm

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:24.580
and the head of Meta

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.030
and I'm really excited
to be talking to him

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:30.940
because I really want to
get into the metaverse

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:31.970
and the Web 3.0 world.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.170
Obviously so much of
this community is aware

00:00:36.081 --> 00:00:39.410
of my involvement in the NFT space.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.290
And it's funny on the way
here I realized, oh my God,

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.960
this interview is going to
go similar to the Joe Namath

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.960
interview I had for all
my sports nerd friends

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.000
that listen to this podcast.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.700
When I had Namath on,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.590
I literally asked him 14
questions that were so left field

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.610
compared to the normal
Joe Namath questions.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.870
For example, I asked him about why

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.710
we lost the AFC championship game

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:05.450
and didn't go to Super Bowl IV

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.712
instead of talking about Super Bowl III.

00:01:07.712 --> 00:01:09.310
And I realize that's
what's going to happen here

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:14.310
because I have so many very
selfish questions for Mark

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:18.940
about this Web 3.0 world
that I'm going to be selfish

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.160
with my limited time with him.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.630
So Mark, first of all,
it's good to see you.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.180
And how are you?

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.883
- It's good to see you too.

00:01:26.722 --> 00:01:28.680
It's been a while since we've
done something like this,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.610
but I'm glad to get a chance to do it.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.460
There's a lot of exciting
stuff going on right now.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.060
You know the reason why we come to you

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.940
is because you do ask good questions.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:38.773
So let's go for it.

00:01:38.773 --> 00:01:40.640
I'm curious what you got for me.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.070
- So, Mark, I think what's really funny

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:46.520
in the Meta, pun intended,
it's NFT NYC right now.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.620
And I was saying to AJ and Kevin Rose,

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:51.070
bunch of the old Web 2.0 friends,

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:55.490
how much this feels like the
early South by Southwest days.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.680
And obviously, one of the
first times we sat down

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.060
was after your keynote at South

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.550
by just jamming ,talking about the space.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:06.510
And I remember the conversation vividly

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:07.570
'cause I have a solid memory

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:09.630
and 'cause a lot of the
things we talked about

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.820
about where this was
all going was the topic.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.040
And many of these things played out.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.370
As somebody that I feel has always had

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.980
a disproportionate understanding of

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.433
communication, consumer, human behavior,

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.470
give me your first hot take.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:31.140
I know you've had this big
announcement the other day.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.000
What is your macro thesis
of the Web 3.0 place

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.820
we're going, whether it's
the metaverse, the NFT stuff,

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:39.790
like what are we on the precipice of

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:43.230
and how similar is it to the time

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.913
where right before Facebook was launched?

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.070
- Yeah, so the metaverse to me today

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:54.530
feels like the next frontier
in social connection

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:56.620
in much the same way that
social networking did

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.610
when I was getting started back in 2004.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.730
That's a big reason why we
wanted to change the brand

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.370
of the company is that
today I think most people

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:08.900
think about us as a social media company,

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.840
but in our DNA, we're a technology company

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.640
that builds all kinds
of different technology

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:14.560
to help people connect

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.290
and tries to advance human connection.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.540
And of course, social media
is one important part of that.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.470
But I think increasingly
it's going to be about

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.250
building platforms and experiences

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:26.240
that deliver the sense of presence.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:27.900
Like you're right there
with another person.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.570
There's, of course, all the
virtual and augmented reality

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:31.403
parts of that.

00:03:31.403 --> 00:03:32.236
And there's the hardware.

00:03:32.236 --> 00:03:33.740
And I'm really excited about that.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:36.770
The work that we've been working on that

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.230
for seven years now at this point.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:39.980
So that's making a lot of progress.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.380
But I think some of the experiences

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.080
are starting to come together too.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.350
We've started to release
Horizon and Workrooms

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:50.290
and some of these experiences
where you can feel like

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:52.730
you're present with someone in a place.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:57.380
It's just pretty crazy to
see how that's taking off.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:58.680
It's not just games,

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.640
games I think is the
natural starting point.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.860
But beyond that, we're
starting to see at this point

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.800
that social interaction
and just hanging out

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.360
is starting to become the biggest way

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.410
that people spend time on these platforms.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:14.023
That kind of makes sense and
fits with my experience so far.

00:04:14.023 --> 00:04:16.700
- Mark, when you say these platforms,

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.440
are you speaking
specifically in the behaviors

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.380
seen of people in Oculus?

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:22.333
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:26.070
- Let's actually go right there

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.780
because you said something in there

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.230
and I hope the audience is listening.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:30.583
Seven years.

00:04:31.947 --> 00:04:36.947
I remember like yesterday
you, Meta, acquiring Oculus.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:45.180
- Always tough to know how to
refer to it in the past tense.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.680
- I'm gonna, I'm gonna use
both, so stick with me.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:50.320
The Oculus purchase was really interesting

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.760
because the Instagram purchase,

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.510
which I was really kind of caught up in

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.710
because of some of the
content I was making,

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.230
the attempt on Snap,

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.423
all of those made a ton of sense

00:04:58.423 --> 00:05:00.300
because you were executing on the thing

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.860
that I've always thought you had,

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.900
which is where's the current attention?

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.690
How do we play within that space?

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:08.030
The Oculus one was weird for me

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:12.490
because I was like, oh, that's far away.

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:14.403
Why did he do that?

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.740
Seven years in metaverse,
people are just now

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:20.910
starting to kind of get going.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.680
What was the thinking of that?

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:25.570
Like, why'd you do that?

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:28.150
- Well, I mean, a lot of it is just that

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:31.300
we spend most of our
days building social apps

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:33.660
that you use on a little phone.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:36.080
And you know, as powerful as that is,

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.280
you have your phone with you all the time,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.620
it's also pretty limiting.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:41.190
You're not delivering an experience

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:43.370
where you can really feel like
you're with another person.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.150
And in a lot of ways,

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.310
that's sort of the ultimate dream

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.350
of building these digital
social experiences

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.680
is actually being able to make
it so that people can feel

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.780
like they're there together
and doing something together

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.972
and then kind of collaborating.

00:05:55.972 --> 00:05:59.670
And no technology that we
have today can deliver that.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:04.180
So we've seen this progression where,

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:06.280
when I started the company, it was people,

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:08.650
primarily the internet,
was primarily about text.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.900
So people can turn text into a computer.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.900
Then we got phones that had cameras.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.860
So the internet became a
lot more visual and mobile.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:17.960
And over the last few years,

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.860
internet connections have gotten
a lot better for everyone.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:21.580
So video is really the primary way

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:22.680
that we share experiences.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:26.570
So you have this progression
from text to photos to videos,

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:28.570
connection and expressing ourselves

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:31.810
keeps on getting more
natural and immersive,

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:33.680
but that's not the end of the line.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.080
There's going to be something after video

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.000
and it's going to be much more immersive

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.010
and it's going to be
something that we can do

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:39.843
throughout the day.

00:06:39.843 --> 00:06:42.450
So you'll have virtual reality
for when you want to go

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.810
into a really immersive zone,

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.460
you'll have augmented
reality to have holograms,

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:50.250
so you can imagine a
version of this conversation

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:54.130
three or five years from now
where instead of doing this

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:57.440
over video, you're a hologram
here in my living room,

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.260
or I'm a hologram in your living room.

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:00.280
And that's going to be pretty wild.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:03.530
- On that point, I want to jump into that.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:06.280
Do you think, and you
know I've watched you talk

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.580
in the past that I know
how I communicate this

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:11.420
because it's always so challenging.

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:15.592
Is your intuition that it is
three to five years from now

00:07:15.592 --> 00:07:18.270
that the tech between 5G,

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.387
which was an important step
between some of the stuff

00:07:20.387 --> 00:07:22.310
you're doing and other people,

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.310
other companies and
entrepreneurs are doing,

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.450
do you think we can actually...

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:28.790
I saw something I think in my feed

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:31.220
where you were fencing with somebody

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.200
as part of the announcement,

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:34.620
which looked wild 'cause it was on some

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.243
Obi-Wan Kenobi shit.

00:07:37.637 --> 00:07:38.470
Like right?

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:40.080
I was like, oh my God, it's happening!`

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.080
Is that, do you think three
to five is a solid guess?

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.220
Is that optimistic?

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:48.439
Talk to me about that.

00:07:48.439 --> 00:07:49.570
- I think you want to break it down to

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:51.010
there's the virtual reality side

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:52.690
and the augmented reality side.

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.560
VR is here.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:56.670
I think Quest was really the form factor

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.970
that was necessary to make it mainstream.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:02.600
Quest Two I think was a
meaningful step beyond that

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:04.560
and is kind of the first mainstream hit

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:07.697
that we've had so many millions of that...

00:08:09.219 --> 00:08:10.550
- I don't know what you're
allowed to share or not.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:13.620
So if you don't feel
comfortable, tell me you can't.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:14.900
Is there public...

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:15.733
I'm just trying to learn how much...

00:08:15.733 --> 00:08:18.100
- We don't have a public
number yet, but it's...

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:21.860
What I can say is it's many millions

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:26.340
and it's multiple times
more than Quest One,

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:29.190
which was sort of the form
factor we felt like we needed.

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:31.880
So that'll keep on improving

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:34.620
and we'll keep on shipping
new versions of that.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.080
So there were a lot of
great experiences there

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:37.960
and it's been really cool
to see the use cases there

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:40.540
broaden out from games to social

00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:42.610
to now having things around fitness.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:45.470
- It's a lot of fitness,
that caught my radar,

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:48.580
that there was a lot more
people paying for fitness apps

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:50.830
in Quest than I had any clue of.

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:51.790
- Oh yeah, yeah.

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:53.190
I mean, well think about it.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.390
It's kind of like the Peloton model where

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:59.140
instead of having a bike or a treadmill,

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:01.370
you just have your $300 headset

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:02.670
and you can take it with
you anywhere you want.

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:06.120
You can do boxing or dancing
or different kinds of cardio.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:06.953
It's pretty awesome.

00:09:06.953 --> 00:09:08.530
So I think that that'll go for awhile

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:09.560
and get extended a lot.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:11.810
So there all these
different use cases in VR.

00:09:13.044 --> 00:09:14.960
When you're talking
about the fencing video

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:18.520
that I showed with Lee
Kiefer in the hologram,

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:20.980
that, you're going to need
augmented reality glasses.

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:25.230
And that's a harder problem because

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:27.820
first, you're inventing a
completely new optical stack.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:29.980
So you're not just using normal screens

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:32.070
and kind of building an
architecture around that,

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.230
which is how virtual reality
has sort of worked to date.

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:39.230
You need to design a projector
and a set of wave guides

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:41.720
so that way you can have
glasses that look normal,

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:43.460
you can see through them.

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:46.330
There's a lot of interesting
science and engineering there,

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:48.350
but for augmented reality,

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:49.930
if you're going to wear
it throughout the day,

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:52.010
it's just a lot more
important that the form factor

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:54.310
is like normal looking glasses.

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:55.938
I mean, virtual reality you
can do in your living room.

00:09:55.938 --> 00:09:58.880
- Do you think, I'm sorry to interrupt,

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:02.660
do you think by the time it hits scale

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:05.720
that there's a chance that
this is a contact lens game,

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:08.500
not a cumbersome overlay,

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.060
like a glasses or a thick...

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:12.630
what's your take on that?

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:13.490
Is that too hard?

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.590
- I mean, some people are working on that.

00:10:15.590 --> 00:10:18.710
I think that that's quite a
bit further off just because,

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:19.543
think about it.

00:10:19.543 --> 00:10:21.660
It's like whatever is projecting the image

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:23.580
needs to have an internet connection.

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:24.963
It needs to be powered.

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:29.060
I've seen some people have
a model of a contact lens

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:30.980
that has a little projector
that's sort of like

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:32.720
in front of your pupil and your blind spot

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:34.130
and it can project something in,

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:36.710
but then how are you
going to have that sync

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:38.050
with the whole rest of the internet

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:39.750
and be powered throughout the day?

00:10:41.088 --> 00:10:43.853
- What if I swallowed a 5G pill?

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:48.310
- I don't know, let's
say 20 years from now.

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:49.830
I think that that might be a thing.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:51.260
But AR glasses,

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:54.277
I think we're going to start
seeing things that look like

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:58.600
normal looking glasses, but
that can project holograms

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:02.670
into the world within the next five years.

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:05.510
I think that's a somewhat
conservative estimate.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:09.300
- Did Pokemon Go, going
back five years ironically,

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.780
was that something you watched carefully?

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:13.780
Because I was like, holy
shit, this is now happening.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:16.240
People are pulling off on this highway,

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:19.110
jumping into the woods to find a Pikachu.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:19.943
I would have lost...

00:11:19.943 --> 00:11:22.540
I'm pretty good at this game too,

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:24.040
but I would have lost this bet,

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:27.580
which was after that was such
a smash hit through the phone,

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:29.440
the fact that we're here five years later

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:31.980
and there has not been another significant

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:35.170
AR phone execution of that scale,

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:36.650
surprising to me.

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:37.860
What's your take on that?

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:41.537
- Well, I think Pokemon
Go, it was interesting

00:11:41.537 --> 00:11:45.340
and it's a real hit and it is phenomenon,

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:49.160
I consider it to be more
of a location-based game

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:51.020
than an augmented reality game.

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:53.860
The fact that it shows that you look at it

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:55.330
through your camera,

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:56.510
I think is somewhat incidental.

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:59.130
I think the core mechanic is
that you're going to a place.

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:01.560
And so you can do that with
augmented reality or not,

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.520
but there is certainly is
going to be a whole class

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:05.430
of experiences that are like that.

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:08.160
But in terms of things that really

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:12.110
kind of augment reality,
I think you have filters,

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:14.840
face filters, different effects like that,

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:17.580
like what you see in
Instagram and Snapchat.

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:19.080
I think that that's a real thing

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:21.383
that I think is real augmented reality.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:25.810
And certainly I think that
there's going to be a lot more

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:29.940
opportunity there once you get
to these real looking glasses

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:31.470
that can put holograms in the world.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:34.360
So yeah, I would hope that
by the middle of this decade,

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:37.610
we can have something that's
sort of like that fencing clip

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:38.443
that I showed.

00:12:38.443 --> 00:12:40.450
Now, the other issue on
that is you need haptics.

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:44.150
That way, when your sword
hits the hologram sword,

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:46.200
you get a sense of feeling from that.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:48.470
So that's a whole interesting
other area of research,

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:51.310
and I'm not sure exactly
where that will be

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:52.540
by the middle of this decade,

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:54.020
but that's another thing
that we're working on

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:56.170
because it's clearly an important part

00:12:56.170 --> 00:12:58.930
of the whole picture is
you need to be able to,

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:00.720
whether you're playing basketball

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:02.610
or giving someone a high five

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:03.520
or shaking their hand,

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:06.120
you want to be able to get
a sense of pressure back.

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:09.270
That, I think, is just going
to be an important part

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:10.690
of the whole thing too.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:14.250
- Mark, when you make a move like this

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:17.743
to get the organization
to this next place,

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:22.330
I assume, 'cause I think
about when I do things,

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:24.810
oftentimes it's more
for my team internally

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:26.070
than for the world.

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:27.330
Like if they don't understand

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.800
where I'm trying to
take Veyner or my stuff,

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:31.500
then I've got no shot.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:35.100
Was that a thought about
this in a lot of ways?

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:36.470
Like, Hey, I got to make sure,

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:38.407
'cause you're a massive company now.

00:13:38.407 --> 00:13:41.700
Is this like, I need to
give everybody a north star

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.150
internally of like, look, no,
no, this is what we're doing.

00:13:44.150 --> 00:13:48.080
It's not just refining the
algo or a filter on Insta

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:49.470
or something of that nature.

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.200
- Yeah, you get this,

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:53.530
'cause you're running a company here

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:58.530
and a lot of this is really
just about making sure

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:00.660
that our team knows what we're doing.

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.990
Running a company is about
setting prioritization

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:05.093
and principles for where you want to go.

00:14:06.352 --> 00:14:08.920
And I do think that it's
the case that a lot of times

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:12.030
the most effective way to
communicate to the organization

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:13.480
a level of commitment to something

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:14.710
is to go say it externally,

00:14:14.710 --> 00:14:17.132
because now people know
that you're serious.

00:14:17.132 --> 00:14:21.470
So we've been talking about
this internally for many years.

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:25.200
We've been working on these
VR devices for seven years.

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:28.200
We've just sort of steadily
ramped up the investment

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:29.700
to the point where now, in 2021,

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:31.800
where we're investing $10 billion,

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.445
more than $10 billion in this.

00:14:33.445 --> 00:14:35.960
It's still not the biggest
part of what we do,

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:37.470
but it's very meaningful.

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:39.190
I think that you'd be hard pressed

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:42.060
to find any other organization
that cares as much

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:45.650
about this and is putting
as much energy into building

00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:47.590
all these different parts of the future.

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:49.340
And what I think you get for that

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:53.600
is that Meta has become the premier place

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:55.490
that if you care about these problems,

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:57.010
you want to go work on them.

00:14:57.010 --> 00:14:57.920
So whether that's VR,

00:14:57.920 --> 00:14:58.920
we're building the best device.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:01.000
AR, I think we're the furthest ahead

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:04.850
in terms of actually building
the consumer glass form factor

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:06.680
and all the different
research around that.

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:08.620
You mentioned any of the
other problems around that,

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:12.250
whether it's haptics or
a lot of the software

00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:14.300
parts of this, where people can interact.

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:18.320
We're going to be the
place where you can build

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:20.300
all these different parts
of the metaverse experience

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:23.295
and then also weave them
into Facebook and Instagram

00:15:23.295 --> 00:15:24.309
and WhatsApp.

00:15:24.309 --> 00:15:25.720
That's going to be pretty wild.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:27.720
- You said something that
really caught my attention.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:29.630
Obviously the far majority of people,

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:31.400
almost everybody who's listening to this

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:32.233
that runs a company,

00:15:32.233 --> 00:15:35.030
is not going to be running
a company of your scale.

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:38.490
But what you said there
was not only for your team,

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:41.960
but did I hear the undertone
there of the fact that

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:44.340
you put out such a public
commitment to this,

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:47.730
you see as a recruiting vehicle

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:50.590
to the best engineers in
the game in this space?

00:15:50.590 --> 00:15:51.423
- Yeah. Well, look.

00:15:51.423 --> 00:15:53.050
I just think that when you plant a flag

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:55.020
and say that you're gonna go do something,

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:57.440
you get haters and criticisms,

00:15:57.440 --> 00:15:59.890
you also get the people who
actually care about that thing

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:03.410
and want to make it happen
are attracted to the people

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:05.800
who I think have the courage to go say,

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:06.680
I'm gonna go make this happen

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:07.850
even though it's really far off.

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:10.140
So I think that all of these things are,

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:12.290
there are pros and cons to them,

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:14.350
and there's a lot of complexity to manage,

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:15.190
but I think that this is true

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:18.020
no matter what size organization you're at

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:21.820
is that having the
willingness to just go say,

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:23.820
Hey, here's what I want to go build,

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:26.860
I think certainly creates
the self-selection dynamic,

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.890
where when you say here's who
I am and what I want to go do,

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:31.790
then you get people
who want to share that.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:35.040
And what I've found in my career

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:37.790
is that it's better to
not be timid about that

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:42.090
and to not pretend that you're
something that you're not

00:16:42.090 --> 00:16:44.170
to try to appease critics.

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:46.140
The more that you can just
be honest to who you are

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:47.410
and what you want to go do,

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:49.000
I think you'll kind of
get the right people

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:49.833
to join your team.

00:16:49.833 --> 00:16:50.710
You'll get the right investors,

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:51.670
you'll get the right partners.

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:54.870
And I think that that's kind
of how you move forward.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.360
- Talk to me now, I'm going crazy selfish.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:00.780
I referenced you and the
company a ton in my content

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:03.900
over the last decade as my macro thesis,

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:08.220
understand where the attention
is, it matters so much.

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:11.890
And I often reference your M&amp;A behavior.

00:17:11.890 --> 00:17:14.660
Can you just give me, because
I want to know for myself,

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:18.900
the insight on the Instagram
deal, the WhatsApp deal,

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:20.460
I'm going to leave Oculus off,

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:22.620
because that was like, I
want to maybe come back

00:17:22.620 --> 00:17:25.840
to it in a little bit given
the macro metaverse convo,

00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:29.300
but specifically Instagram and WhatsApp,

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:32.500
which I thought were
really of the moment deals.

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:34.690
I don't know if the corporation

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:36.895
acknowledges the attempt towards Snapchat.

00:17:36.895 --> 00:17:39.500
It was well talked about,

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:41.600
I don't know the truth or not truth to it,

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:45.490
when I heard it or when it
was reported, I was like,

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:46.530
there he is again.

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:47.550
He's a hundred percent right.

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:49.003
He's on it again.

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:54.590
TikTok must've been potentially
a very different thing,

00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:57.100
given they're China-based,
the complications...

00:17:59.573 --> 00:18:01.110
I'll be very honest.

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:04.350
every day when I saw
Musical.ly way back, I'm like,

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.240
Facebook's gonna make a play.

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:06.690
Facebook's gonna make a play.

00:18:08.341 --> 00:18:09.780
A, am I right

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:11.730
that is a big tenor of how you guys

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:13.310
have looked at the world?

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:15.223
B, what's that about?

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:17.193
- Yeah.

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:20.100
So there are a lot of different

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:24.190
of these kind of core social interactions

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:26.530
that people have in these apps

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:29.810
that the different apps
have invented over time.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:33.350
And I'm proud that that we've
invented a bunch of them,

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:36.360
going back to some of
the core dynamics around

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:38.601
being able to communicate
with people in your college,

00:18:38.601 --> 00:18:40.683
we were the first that built newsfeed,

00:18:41.550 --> 00:18:43.801
the core social API work early on...

00:18:43.801 --> 00:18:44.634
- By way, on the record
for the youngsters,

00:18:44.634 --> 00:18:48.480
I apologize.

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:52.010
By the way, for all the
youngsters listening right now,

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:56.400
when Facebook changed from
you going to somebody's wall

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:59.560
and leaving a comment, to this newsfeed,

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:02.560
it was fucking carnage.

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:05.450
The number one page or group
or whatever you guys called it

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:08.640
back then was bringing
back the wall or whatever.

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:10.730
Mark, can you speak to that real quick?

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:12.850
Just go back to your young, young days,

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:15.050
like when you guys made that move,

00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:16.200
the community within,

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:17.570
nobody was even paying attention to you

00:19:17.570 --> 00:19:19.590
in the mainstream and all that stuff now.

00:19:19.590 --> 00:19:23.420
Within the world, immediately
people lost their mind.

00:19:23.420 --> 00:19:25.450
And now that is the core
way every social network

00:19:25.450 --> 00:19:27.140
was built in.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:29.840
- Yeah. I mean, look,
sometimes when you invent

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:34.010
these things, they can be disruptive.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:36.160
And I think you need
to have the commitment

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:37.630
to see it through.

00:19:37.630 --> 00:19:39.710
But going back to your point about

00:19:39.710 --> 00:19:40.583
some of these other companies,

00:19:40.583 --> 00:19:43.780
it's like there was a
kernel with Instagram

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:47.250
and with WhatsApp and also with companies

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:51.550
like Snapchat, I think
that they created something

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:54.640
that I think is really
special and is awesome.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:56.980
I just looked at that and I
was like, all right, okay.

00:19:56.980 --> 00:19:57.930
There is something,

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:01.340
I think people often tend
to look at these social apps

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:03.210
and think that they're
frivolous, early on,

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:04.840
and they think that they're like

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:06.863
these dynamics aren't important.

00:20:06.863 --> 00:20:09.290
Oh, it's filtered photos or

00:20:09.290 --> 00:20:12.310
oh, it's disappearing photos

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:14.739
or whatever it is.

00:20:14.739 --> 00:20:17.940
- Or it's only for college kids.

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:19.570
- Yeah, when we had that,

00:20:19.570 --> 00:20:21.010
through our IPO and after

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:24.850
when we were having a lot
of business struggles,

00:20:26.169 --> 00:20:28.020
but I kind of looked at
those and I was like,

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:31.540
Hey, I think that there's
something that's important here.

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:34.520
I think the world is probably
underestimating this.

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:37.720
And I also think that we
have the skills as a company

00:20:37.720 --> 00:20:39.950
to go grow these things

00:20:39.950 --> 00:20:42.390
to reach more than a billion
people around the world.

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:44.210
And because we'd done that with the core

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:45.360
Facebook experience.

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:47.500
And I think that there's two skills there,

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:50.440
there's sort of the building
the social experience

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:53.070
and then there's the
helping to ramp up a network

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:53.903
around that.

00:20:53.903 --> 00:20:56.550
And that I think is
also a core competence.

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:58.500
I don't know what would
have happened with Instagram

00:20:58.500 --> 00:20:59.380
if we hadn't bought it.

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:01.140
I don't think it's guaranteed
that it would have grown

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:02.160
to be as big as it is.

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:02.993
- Should I assume,

00:21:02.993 --> 00:21:04.640
'cause I'm a nerd when it comes to

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:06.250
watching business behavior,

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:08.850
that much like what you're
doing with metaverse now,

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:10.990
which I think is the macro move of that,

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:14.480
and that in between now
and that scaled world,

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:19.000
the companies that have
the kernels that do best

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:21.746
in the attention graph, whether
that's in the metaverse,

00:21:21.746 --> 00:21:25.773
video, picture, that will
always be a core focus.

00:21:26.890 --> 00:21:27.980
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:30.860
I think that that's a key
thing with this rebrand to Meta

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:33.578
is it's not like now we're
not focused on social media.

00:21:33.578 --> 00:21:35.340
That's going to be the bread
and butter of what we do.

00:21:35.340 --> 00:21:37.140
That's the core thing.

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:38.870
And our work to build the metaverse

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:41.190
encompasses both building
social experiences

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:44.760
and building these future
platforms like VR and AR.

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:45.790
It's gotta be both.

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:47.860
We have to weave all of
these new technologies

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:49.630
through these social apps,

00:21:49.630 --> 00:21:51.530
'cause you want to be able
to jump into the metaverse

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:54.110
and a 3D experience from
your Instagram feed.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:56.280
So you see your friend at a concert,

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:58.430
we showed this as part of
the keynote presentation,

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:01.570
and just dive in and maybe
be a hologram at the concert.

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:03.670
But a lot of the discovery around that

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:06.000
is going to happen through
the core social platforms.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:09.053
So yeah, that's gonna
continue to be a focus.

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:12.800
We're going to keep on focusing
on growing and building apps

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:15.470
and adding more social
mechanics around that.

00:22:15.470 --> 00:22:17.910
I think there's a lot
more to invent there.

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:20.480
And then I think that there's
this next set of platforms,

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:23.920
where one of the things that I reflect on

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:27.360
a lot is that social media kind of grew up

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:28.939
with the smartphone.

00:22:28.939 --> 00:22:32.910
Facebook, I started it in 2004.

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:34.500
I think Apple was probably
already working on

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:35.670
their iPhone designed by then.

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:37.880
It came out in 2007.

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:41.040
We didn't really get to shape
what the smartphone was.

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:44.010
We built a lot of the most
used experiences for it,

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:47.460
but it grew at the same time.

00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:48.860
And because of that,

00:22:48.860 --> 00:22:51.790
I feel like the smartphone
sort of grew in a way

00:22:51.790 --> 00:22:54.580
that it's somewhat limiting
in terms of the type

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:56.310
of social experiences that you could have.

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:57.550
I feel like there's a lot of things

00:22:57.550 --> 00:22:59.710
that I would love to
build that we can't build

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:02.780
because we're kind of
constrained into this

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:06.320
little rectangle and policies
that some other company's set.

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:09.470
So that's part of, for me,

00:23:09.470 --> 00:23:12.350
why I have so much
passionate about helping

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:14.710
to bring about this next platform shift

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:17.120
and accelerating it because
I think the sooner we get to

00:23:17.120 --> 00:23:19.640
virtual and augmented reality, the better,

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:21.950
the more magical these social
experiences are gonna be.

00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:25.060
And I just think our platforms

00:23:25.060 --> 00:23:26.400
should be designed around people,

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:27.233
interacting with each other.

00:23:27.233 --> 00:23:29.847
And that's like how we
process the world as people.

00:23:29.847 --> 00:23:32.590
And that's just not how
phones are designed.

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:34.230
They're designed around apps today.

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:36.460
But I think, going back
to your first question

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:39.723
about NFTs and Web 3.0,

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:43.810
I think the atomic unit in the metaverse

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:48.447
is going to be about you and your stuff

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:51.420
and your friends and your connection.

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:53.000
So you're gonna have your avatar

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:54.950
and you're gonna have
your digital clothing

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:56.570
and your digital tools.

00:23:56.570 --> 00:24:00.380
And unlike apps today
that are all designed

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:01.940
to be a little bit siloed,

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:03.270
and you have to do all this extra work

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:05.160
to get them to work Together,

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:06.950
in the metaverse, I think
it's going to be fundamentally

00:24:06.950 --> 00:24:10.110
more interoperable where
your fundamental experiences

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:12.230
that you are embodied in your identity,

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:14.800
your digital avatar, all your stuff.

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:18.000
And I think as a user of this,

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:19.223
your natural expectation is going to be

00:24:19.223 --> 00:24:21.370
that you can bring all your stuff

00:24:21.370 --> 00:24:23.570
in between all these
experiences very seamlessly.

00:24:23.570 --> 00:24:25.780
So I think that that's
going to be really exciting.

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:27.000
And I just want to help bring that about

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:27.833
as soon as possible.

00:24:27.833 --> 00:24:30.000
- Mark, question I've
been dying to ask you.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.660
When you first look, you probably given,

00:24:32.660 --> 00:24:34.240
and maybe I'm making assumptions,

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:35.400
so you'll speak to it.

00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:38.387
Obviously there was that
2017 wave with CryptoKitties

00:24:38.387 --> 00:24:41.380
and we started seeing
that early wave of NFTs.

00:24:43.870 --> 00:24:45.420
That was like the thing that,

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:46.960
Punks did not hit my radar,

00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:48.757
but CryptoKitties did in '17.

00:24:50.830 --> 00:24:53.330
And obviously now this
has been the year of NFTs,

00:24:53.330 --> 00:24:55.230
the way we know them,
whether it's Bored Ape,

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:56.550
what I do with VeeFriends,

00:24:56.550 --> 00:25:00.780
obviously Punks and Cool Cats,
many, many, many projects.

00:25:00.780 --> 00:25:03.370
One of the reasons it was very easy for me

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:07.190
when I really dug in late last year

00:25:07.190 --> 00:25:09.300
to believe in this was actually because of

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:10.943
Farmville on Facebook.

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:15.490
There was two things that
happened to me during that year.

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:16.560
Farmville on Facebook.

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:19.520
Oh my God, People are
buying these digital sheep

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:22.490
because they want the social
currency to show their friends

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:23.770
they're good at it.

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:25.791
And then Ze Frank.

00:25:25.791 --> 00:25:26.670
I don't know if you remember Ze Frank.

00:25:26.670 --> 00:25:28.130
- Yeah, uh huh.

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:31.080
Ze Franks was one of the
first video bloggers.

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:35.410
He had people buy virtual
ducks as little tip jars

00:25:35.410 --> 00:25:37.170
and have their name hover over it.

00:25:37.170 --> 00:25:39.150
And those were the first times I was like,

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:42.320
oh my God, people are going
to buy virtual things,

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:43.210
virtual currency.

00:25:43.210 --> 00:25:44.360
In my book, Thank you Economy,

00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:47.740
I talk a lot about virtual
currency in 2010, '11.

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:50.060
Was it natural for you to believe in

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:51.640
what's happening right now with NFTs

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:53.480
because of the things like Farmville

00:25:53.480 --> 00:25:55.840
that happened on your platform?

00:25:55.840 --> 00:25:58.820
- Well, I've always been
a pretty big believer

00:25:58.820 --> 00:25:59.960
in virtual goods.

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:01.740
So I think from that perspective, yeah.

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:05.950
But I think a lot of the magic of NFTs

00:26:05.950 --> 00:26:07.500
and a lot of the Web 3.0 work

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:09.880
is that it's designed in a way

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:11.620
to be fundamentally interoperable.

00:26:11.620 --> 00:26:14.010
So I think that that's
going to be really important

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:16.410
because it will help break down the silos

00:26:16.410 --> 00:26:18.360
between different apps and make it so that

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:22.330
all your stuff can be more portable

00:26:22.330 --> 00:26:23.440
between these different experiences,

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:24.870
which I think increasingly is what people

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:25.953
are going to expect.

00:26:27.904 --> 00:26:29.190
I think that that's going to be a big part

00:26:29.190 --> 00:26:32.070
for creators of making it
really worth investing in

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:34.760
because if you're designing a digital good

00:26:35.772 --> 00:26:37.610
for Farmville and it
only works within there,

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:39.173
then like, I dunno.

00:26:40.795 --> 00:26:42.180
You get to the point
where most of the people

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:43.933
who are going to be building that...

00:26:43.933 --> 00:26:47.030
- To your point, here
the world we live in now.

00:26:47.030 --> 00:26:50.970
You're a young kid and you're
in Minecraft or Roblox,

00:26:50.970 --> 00:26:53.000
you grow up and you
want to go to Fortnight.

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:56.810
All that money you
invested is stuck in there.

00:26:56.810 --> 00:26:58.790
In the world that you're
talking about right now,

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:02.570
the Roblox and Minecraft of the future,

00:27:02.570 --> 00:27:03.930
when you're done with that,

00:27:03.930 --> 00:27:06.570
you're just gonna trade it
for your Fortnite stuff.

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:08.880
You're gonna trade the
Fortnite stuff or sell it.

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:12.030
It's all gonna be in that
one global ecosystem.

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:15.450
- Yeah, the analogy that
I like to think about is,

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:16.750
I like your Knicks hoodie,

00:27:17.994 --> 00:27:19.570
but imagine if you bought a Jersey

00:27:19.570 --> 00:27:22.184
and you could only use
it in the sports arena

00:27:22.184 --> 00:27:23.670
where you bought it.

00:27:23.670 --> 00:27:25.720
It's like that would be sort of lame

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:28.503
and it would reduce
the value of buying it.

00:27:29.550 --> 00:27:31.106
Because who's gonna want to buy something

00:27:31.106 --> 00:27:32.800
if they can only use it in that arena?

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:33.890
I mean, some people would,

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:35.720
but a fraction of the number of people

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:36.553
who would want to buy it

00:27:36.553 --> 00:27:38.220
if they could use in all
these different places.

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:40.090
And then if the amount of
commerce is going to be less

00:27:40.090 --> 00:27:42.000
than that's gonna attract fewer creators.

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:44.160
So I think having it be more interoperable

00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:46.370
is going to be key to making
the whole thing so dynamic.

00:27:46.370 --> 00:27:47.820
- What's interesting about that

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:49.900
in a much more interesting
human behavior way

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:54.690
is people will wear it, to
your point, it's limiting.

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:57.840
What's also interesting is the clout,

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:00.500
the equity, the social currency,

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:03.940
carries more weight from a
tribalism behavior standpoint

00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:05.060
in Madison Square Garden,

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:06.590
which I'm looking at right now,

00:28:06.590 --> 00:28:07.960
than it does outside,

00:28:07.960 --> 00:28:10.640
though it has tremendous value outside.

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:13.640
What I'm incredibly interested
in is the following.

00:28:13.640 --> 00:28:16.100
Obviously, I don't
remember if it was Twitter,

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:19.850
I think it was Twitter, who
did the blue check mark first,

00:28:19.850 --> 00:28:21.160
The verification.

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:24.780
We've now lived through a
decade where verified accounts,

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:29.780
following counts, social
equity through visualization.

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:33.903
We've seen it with followers
and verified accounts,

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:39.700
The extremity of the NFT space
is going to be even greater

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:42.190
for what that means.

00:28:42.190 --> 00:28:45.010
It's almost like our world
is all about to become

00:28:45.010 --> 00:28:47.980
the fashion industry because
we communicate so much

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:49.890
through what we wear,

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:51.850
the digital version of
that is going to have

00:28:51.850 --> 00:28:54.640
an incredible impact on society.

00:28:54.640 --> 00:28:55.530
- Oh, totally.

00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:57.560
I think if we're all
spending a lot more time

00:28:57.560 --> 00:29:00.080
in the metaverse, then I
think we're going to care

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:03.680
a lot about our
representation of our identity

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:06.497
and we're going to want different outfits.

00:29:06.497 --> 00:29:09.521
Maybe everyone except me,
I just wear the same thing.

00:29:09.521 --> 00:29:11.840
- Did you steal that from Steve Jobs?

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:13.490
Mark, hard hitting question.

00:29:13.490 --> 00:29:16.290
Did you steal wearing
the same thing every day

00:29:16.290 --> 00:29:17.323
from Steve Jobs?

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:19.630
- I don't think so.

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:22.330
I just think I was maybe a little lazy

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:27.330
and then had some good
articulation of like, oh yeah,

00:29:28.270 --> 00:29:30.120
this is just about saving mental energy.

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:32.163
But I think, I don't know.

00:29:32.163 --> 00:29:33.840
It was never really a....

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:36.110
- You know what's funny Mark,
I'll be honest with you.

00:29:36.110 --> 00:29:38.270
I actually think that's the punchline.

00:29:38.270 --> 00:29:41.810
You consciously or
subconsciously made that decision

00:29:41.810 --> 00:29:43.190
to represent yourself.

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:47.233
And I think that going digital
is going to be extraordinary.

00:29:49.090 --> 00:29:49.923
I really do.

00:29:49.923 --> 00:29:52.550
I really think that people
are grossly underestimating

00:29:52.550 --> 00:29:55.000
that almost all the
things we've been doing

00:29:55.000 --> 00:30:00.000
are about to get more
visual, more collateralized,

00:30:00.670 --> 00:30:02.370
more obvious.

00:30:02.370 --> 00:30:03.670
I don't think a lot of people realize

00:30:03.670 --> 00:30:05.200
why they bought a Rolex.

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:06.040
I really don't.

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:07.700
I don't think a lot of people realize.

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:11.570
I don't think I overthink
why, at 45 years old,

00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:14.270
I look like I'm 13 right now.

00:30:14.270 --> 00:30:16.270
There's a lot going on in that.

00:30:16.270 --> 00:30:19.440
And I think that when it hits digital,

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:20.680
it becomes more obvious.

00:30:20.680 --> 00:30:22.820
I think a lot of people's conversation

00:30:22.820 --> 00:30:24.560
about what's going on on digital

00:30:24.560 --> 00:30:28.060
is actually just showing us what we are

00:30:28.060 --> 00:30:29.880
much more than anything else.

00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:34.510
And so I think as we go into
the realm of consumerism

00:30:34.510 --> 00:30:37.860
is insane, when you think
about how much people

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:40.890
buy things to communicate,
that going digital

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:42.910
and being so in our faces,

00:30:42.910 --> 00:30:45.120
because so many of these
things sit in our homes

00:30:45.120 --> 00:30:47.970
where 99% of our social
graph never sees it,

00:30:47.970 --> 00:30:51.913
that going to a full 100 is fucking crazy.

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:54.100
- But I think you're hitting on a number

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:55.980
of important pieces here.

00:30:55.980 --> 00:30:59.560
First, a lot of people think
about this as kind of commerce.

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:02.530
And I think a lot of it is
actually really expression,

00:31:02.530 --> 00:31:04.080
which is the core social dynamics.

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:06.600
So the intersection
between that expression

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:09.020
and your identity, and then
the commerce around that,

00:31:09.020 --> 00:31:11.370
especially if we can get
it to be interoperable

00:31:11.370 --> 00:31:13.010
so it's a more fluid market,

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:15.400
I think is just going
to be a really big deal,

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:19.050
a much bigger than I think
people internalized today.

00:31:19.050 --> 00:31:22.500
The other thing that I
think is really refreshing

00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:24.790
and insightful to hear you talk about is

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:27.500
all the lessons from some
of the previous rounds

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:28.380
of development.

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:30.340
I hadn't really thought
before you raised it

00:31:30.340 --> 00:31:33.720
about some of the early
Farmville experiences

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:35.910
or some of the stuff from a decade ago.

00:31:35.910 --> 00:31:37.160
But I think you're right

00:31:37.160 --> 00:31:38.910
that there is a clear through line,

00:31:38.910 --> 00:31:42.900
both between the social
interactions that people have

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:44.870
and the types of commerce.

00:31:44.870 --> 00:31:46.890
Obviously there's more technology now

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:48.537
that can make it more interoperable

00:31:48.537 --> 00:31:51.700
and that can give people
more rights over the goods

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:52.533
that they have.

00:31:52.533 --> 00:31:54.230
And on the AR and VR side,

00:31:54.230 --> 00:31:56.360
making it so people can
feel a more realistic sense

00:31:56.360 --> 00:31:57.330
of connection.

00:31:57.330 --> 00:31:59.400
But I think you're right,

00:31:59.400 --> 00:32:02.420
that a lot of these concepts
are not fundamentally new.

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:05.980
They're sort of the next
iteration on dynamics

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:10.410
that are evergreen and that
are just always going to be,

00:32:10.410 --> 00:32:12.160
and always have been
very important to people

00:32:12.160 --> 00:32:14.210
in terms of how we connect
and direct with each other.

00:32:14.210 --> 00:32:16.560
- It's my take on what happened
when you were inventing.

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:20.310
I'm like, oh, people
put their kid's school

00:32:20.310 --> 00:32:23.130
on their back of their windshield to flex.

00:32:23.130 --> 00:32:25.443
My kid's going to Harvard
and might drop out.

00:32:26.510 --> 00:32:27.803
That's what they would do.

00:32:28.730 --> 00:32:30.550
We've been doing all these same things,

00:32:30.550 --> 00:32:34.710
digital's just exposing
truths and scaling truths.

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:37.600
And I think the new
frontier of 3.0 metaverse

00:32:37.600 --> 00:32:40.340
is going to take us to a
completely different place

00:32:40.340 --> 00:32:43.383
that I do think Web 2.0
gave us a slight preview to.

00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:46.090
- Yeah, I agree.

00:32:46.090 --> 00:32:46.923
I agree.

00:32:46.923 --> 00:32:49.330
- Mark, since we only have
a couple more minutes,

00:32:51.480 --> 00:32:54.810
do you own any NFTs, have
you thought about buying any?

00:32:54.810 --> 00:32:56.610
I feel like you're going more platforms.

00:32:56.610 --> 00:33:00.350
Obviously, you as the
executive is platform side,

00:33:00.350 --> 00:33:01.610
metaverse, that makes so much sense.

